findings.body:
**Concern:** the new `symlink` fallback in `applyOne` copies the shared tree on hosts where `symlinkSync` throws (e.g. Windows without Developer Mode). That first run leaves `.claude/skills` as a plain directory. On every subsequent run, this precondition fires first:

```ts
if (existsSync(target) || isLink(target)) {
  if (isLink(target)) unlinkSync(target);
  else return "kept (not a symlink)";   // exits here on a plain dir from the earlier copy
}
```

Because the copy is a plain directory (not a link), this returns `"kept (not a symlink)"` and never reaches the `symlinkSync`/`cpSync` fallback logic below it — so the content is never re-synced. Meanwhile `wouldChange()`'s `symlink` case (same file, lines 43-50) *does* detect drift here via `sameTree()`, and `init` calls `apply(plan, root)` unconditionally on every run (not gated by `wouldChange`) — confirmed by tracing `cli.ts:175`. So a Windows user who edits `.agents/skills/` later gets `--check` reporting drift forever, with the tool's own suggested fix ("Run agent-init to bring them back in line") unable to actually fix it; only manually deleting `.claude/skills` first works.

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/agent-init/blob/c4e0d8de9e3323c7635df2cc219109c729c27404/src/apply.utils.ts#L115-L131

**Concern:** the new per-tool skills check:

```ts
// A symlink that materialised as a text file, or a copy that never arrived, leaves a path
// that leads nowhere: the tool then loads no skills at all and says nothing about it.
const skills = SKILL_PATHS[tool];
if (skills) {
  const dir = path.join(root, skills);
  const reachable = existsSync(dir) && readdirSync(dir).length > 0;
  ...
}
```

`existsSync(dir)` returns `true` for any existing path regardless of type, so it doesn't guard against `dir` being a regular file. `readdirSync` on a regular file throws `ENOTDIR` synchronously (standard Node `fs` behavior). There is no `try`/`catch` anywhere between this code and the top-level `process.exit(await main(...))` call, so in the exact "symlink materialised as a text file" case the comment names, `doctor` crashes with an unhandled exception instead of emitting a clean `FAIL` line.

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/agent-init/blob/c4e0d8de9e3323c7635df2cc219109c729c27404/src/cli.ts#L261-L274

findings.suggestion:
const skills = SKILL_PATHS[tool];
if (skills) {
  const dir = path.join(root, skills);
  let entries: string[] = [];
  try {
    entries = readdirSync(dir);
  } catch {
    entries = [];
  }
  const reachable = entries.length > 0;
  checks.push({
    name: `${tool} skills`,
    ok: reachable,
    detail: reachable
      ? `${entries.length} entr(ies) under ${skills}`
      : `${skills} resolves to nothing — the tool will load no skills`,
  });
}
